diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk report that PC0 (20.1.1.10) cannot reach PC12 (120.1.1.10) is not confirmed by any live evidence. A topology-wide reachability test in Phase 1 shows 0% packet loss in both directions, and every intermediate hop and protocol on the alleged fault path is fully operational.

**Affected component:** None — the OSPF-to-EIGRP redistribution boundary at R11 and all interior links are healthy.

**Mechanism (why the report is unconfirmed):** R11 runs FRR ospfd (area 0.0.0.3, interface eth1 at 111.1.1.2/8) and FRR eigrpd (AS 100, networks 112.0.0.0/8 toward R12 and 103.0.0.0/8 toward R13), with `redistribute eigrp` configured in its OSPF process. R12 runs EIGRP AS 100 and advertises 120.0.0.0/8 (the segment containing PC12). R11 has active EIGRP adjacencies to both R12 (112.1.1.2) and R13 (103.1.1.2), and R1's routing table confirms 120.0.0.0/8 reachable via 111.1.1.2 (R11) as an OSPF external route — proving the end-to-end redistribution chain is intact. l2_snapshot, infra_sweep, tc_snapshot, and pressure_sweep all return clean. The ospf_snapshot flags on R13 (ospfd not running) and R12 (ospfd unconfigured) are expected artifacts of those two being pure EIGRP routers; no real OSPF adjacency anomaly exists. R8's orphaned area 0.0.0.2 is a single-router area whose loopback routes propagate correctly and is not on the PC0-PC12 path.

**Localization:** No device implicated.

**Root cause:** The reported cross-core fault does not exist in the current lab state; no fault mechanism or historical trace (no flap process, no carrier changes, no daemon crash, no ACL rule) was found on any device.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. If the ticket describes a past transient, capture FRR eigrpd logs on R11 during recurrence before escalating further.
